  "page1": {
    "title": "Building and Taking Apart Number Machines",
    "subtitle": "Why expanding and factoring are the same skill in reverse.",
    "body": "Think of a <u>polynomial</u> as a little machine made of number blocks. Something like (x + 3)(x + 2) is just two small blocks waiting to be joined. When you multiply them out step by step, you get x squared plus 5x plus 6. This building process is called <mark>expand</mark>ing the polynomial. You took two simple parts and turned them into one longer expression. Every term came from a clear rule: you multiply each piece in the first block by each piece in the second, then add the results.\n\nFactoring is the very same skill, but it runs backward. Suppose someone hands you x squared plus 5x plus 6 and asks where it came from. You then try to find the two original blocks that built it in the first place. You look for two numbers that multiply to 6 and add up to 5. Those numbers are 3 and 2, so the machine breaks neatly back into (x + 3)(x + 2). Expanding builds the machine, while factoring <u>takes it apart</u>. Because the two moves are mirror images, you can always check one by doing the other.\n\nThis back-and-forth between building and breaking is really the heart of school algebra. Once you can factor with confidence, many hard problems suddenly get easy. A long, scary equation can shrink into a few simple pieces, and those pieces often hand you the answers right away. Engineers, coders, and economists all lean on the same trick whenever they simplify messy formulas. The real lesson here is not just the steps but the habit of looking for structure. A scary line of symbols is often just a simple machine wearing a clever <blank>, quietly waiting for someone patient enough to take it apart.",
    "illustration": "../../assets/illustrations/2026-07/02.jpg",
    "illustration_caption": "Expanding joins the blocks; factoring breaks them back apart.",
    "gloss_extra": [
      {
        "term": "factoring",
        "ko": "인수분해"
      },
